  "query_preview": ".. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nAn unseasonably strong synoptic system continues to work its way\nthru the Northeast today. A deep upper trof and strong surface\nlow for this time of year will spin thru the Gulf of Maine into\nthe evening before finally drifting east. \n\nLatest radar trends show limited rainfall across the area this\nmorning and the threat for flooding has diminished. CAMs have\nlargely struggled with handling rainfall overnight and show\nvariability in showers redeveloping today. Models generally\nsuggest showers and perhaps some thunder blossoms across SE NH\nand the coastal plain of Maine from late morning into the\nafternoon that could bring localized heavy rainfall. Low \npressure moving east and the loss of daytime heating will see \nshowers and storms really diminish quickly this evening.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nBy Fri conditions will have improve, but the region will remain\nbeneath lower heights aloft. That will generally mean clearer\nskies early in the day followed by diurnal increase in cloud\ncover and the chance for scattered showers and storms. The same\nwill be true of Sat, with any shower activity more widely\nscattered. \n\nBy Sun the next trof will be approaching. Upper flow will be\nsouthwesterly, more or less parallel to the frontal boundary. So\nI do not expect it to be moving very fast. As such I have no\nissue with NBM PoP being spread out over two days, into Mon. It\nis also possible that this front will be a play in two acts. Act\none being convection forming Sun and falling apart over western\nzones, and act two with convection developing again Mon slightly\neast and departing the area by late in the day.",
